<report>
<title>Chi-square test (2 by k)</title>
<br />
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Successes</th><th>Failures</th><th>Total</th><th>Per cent</th><th>Score</th></tr>
<block name="row">
<tr><td>Observed</td><td>@obs_succ</td><td>@obs_fail</td><td>@obs_tot</td><td>@obs_pc</td><td>@score</td></tr>
<tr><td><model>Expected</model></td><td><model>@exp_succ</model></td><td><model>@exp_fail</model></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
</block><tr><th>Total</th><th>@tot_succ</th><th>@tot_fail</th><th>@tot_tot</th><th>@tot_pc</th></tr>
</table>
<block name="warn">

<p><warn>Warning: @num out of @den cells have expected frequency less than 5</warn></p>
</block>

<p>Total Chi&#xb2; = @chi  |Chi| = @chi_abs (@totdf DF)  <pval>@{chi_p:pval}</pval></p>
<block name="z">

<p>Chi&#xb2; for linear trend = @chi_lin  |Chi| = @chi_1df (1 DF)  <pval>@{chi_lin_p:pval}</pval></p>

<p>Remaining Chi&#xb2; (non-linearity) = @chi_non (@df DF)  <pval>@{chi_non_p:pval}</pval></p>
</block>
<br />
<br />
</report>
